(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-nephrology-urinary-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"Nephrology / Urinary System — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 3)","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The tubes that transport urine from the kidneys to the urinary bladder are the:","choices":["Collecting ducts","Urethra","Ureters","Renal pelvises"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ureters are muscular tubes that transport urine from the renal pelvis of each kidney down to the urinary bladder. — Slides 3–4 (Organs Involved)"},{"q":"The last part of the urethra, where urine exits the body, is called the:","choices":["The internal urethral orifice itself","The tip of the renal papilla","External urethral meatus (orifice)","The ureterovesical junction point"],"answer":2,"exp":"The external urethral meatus (orifice) is the terminal opening of the urethra where urine exits the body. — Slide 58 (Urethra)"},{"q":"Which organ serves as the expandable reservoir for urine prior to micturition?","choices":["The urethra itself","The ureter","Renal pelvis","Urinary bladder"],"answer":3,"exp":"The urinary bladder is the expandable muscular reservoir that stores urine until micturition, powered by the detrusor muscle. — Slide 49 (Bladder)"},{"q":"The perinephric (perirenal) fat is best described as:","choices":["Dense irregular connective tissue that directly anchors the kidney in place","A thin fatty layer sandwiched between the renal fascia and the peritoneum","An adipose layer that completely surrounds the kidney for cushioning and insulation","The internal cavity space that houses the renal pelvis and its vessels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Perinephric (perirenal) fat is an adipose layer that completely surrounds the kidney and fibrous capsule, cushioning and insulating it. — Slide 13 (Surrounding Tissues)"},{"q":"The renal sinus, the internal space of the kidney, houses all of the following EXCEPT:","choices":["The renal calyces","The renal pelvis","The renal pyramids","Blood vessels and fat"],"answer":2,"exp":"The renal sinus houses the calyces, renal pelvis, blood/lymphatic vessels, nerves, and fat; the renal pyramids are solid medullary tissue, not sinus contents. — Slide 10 (Gross and Sectional Anatomy)"},{"q":"Approximately how many renal pyramids are typically found in each kidney?","choices":["1 to 3","8 to 15","20 to 30","40 to 50"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each kidney typically contains 8 to 15 renal pyramids within the medulla. — Slide 14 (Coronal Plane Sections)"},{"q":"The renal fascia functions to:","choices":["Anchor the kidney to the posterior wall and peritoneum","Filter blood plasma to remove metabolic waste products","Store urine until it is voluntarily released","Produce erythropoietin in response to hypoxia"],"answer":0,"exp":"The renal fascia is dense irregular connective tissue that anchors the kidney (and its fat) to the posterior abdominal wall and peritoneum. — Slide 13 (Surrounding Tissues)"},{"q":"The afferent arteriole delivers blood directly to which structure?","choices":["The arcuate vein","The peritubular capillaries","The vasa recta","The glomerulus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The afferent arteriole delivers blood directly into the glomerulus, where plasma is filtered into the glomerular (capsular) space. — Slide 16 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"The arcuate arteries are positioned where within the kidney?","choices":["Running along the entire outer wall of the ureter's lumen","Embedded deep within the tips of each renal papilla","Suspended within the fluid-filled renal pelvis itself","Arching over the bases of the renal pyramids (corticomedullary junction)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The arcuate arteries arch along the corticomedullary junction, over the bases of the renal pyramids, giving rise to the interlobular arteries. — Slides 16–17 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"Plasma filtered across the glomerulus enters which space first?","choices":["The terminal collecting duct system downstream","The proximal convoluted tubule directly","The glomerular (capsular) space of Bowman's capsule","The minor calyx located near the papilla tip"],"answer":2,"exp":"Filtered plasma first enters the glomerular (capsular) space of Bowman's capsule before flowing into the PCT. — Slide 16 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"Which capillary network is specifically associated with juxtamedullary nephrons for concentrating urine?","choices":["Fenestrated sinusoids","Peritubular capillaries","Glomerular capillaries","Vasa recta"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vasa recta accompany the long loops of Henle of juxtamedullary nephrons and participate in the countercurrent exchange that concentrates urine. — Slides 18, 30 (Blood Supply / Types of Nephrons)"},{"q":"The loop of Henle originates at the end of which tubule?","choices":["The distal convoluted tubule","The proximal convoluted tubule","The collecting duct near the papilla","Bowman's capsule of the corpuscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The loop of Henle originates at the end of the proximal convoluted tubule and projects toward/into the medulla before returning to the cortex as the DCT. — Slide 39 (Loop of Henle)"},{"q":"Once filtrate reaches the collecting ducts, what happens to its composition?","choices":["It is completely reabsorbed by the collecting ducts","It is filtered again at a second glomerulus site","It re-enters the PCT for further modification","No further modifications occur — it is now officially urine"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the collecting ducts no further modifications occur; the filtrate is now officially urine, draining via papillary ducts into the minor calyces. — Slide 41 (Collecting Tubule & Ducts)"},{"q":"The primary purpose of the long loops of juxtamedullary nephrons is the recovery of:","choices":["Proteins and albumin","Glucose and amino acids only","Water, Na+, and Cl-","Bicarbonate exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"The long loops of juxtamedullary nephrons primarily recover water, Na+, and Cl-, which helps concentrate the urine. — Slide 30 (Types of Nephrons)"},{"q":"Multiple collecting ducts converge to form a structure that drains urine into a minor calyx; this structure is the:","choices":["Renal column","Renal papilla","Renal hilum","Glomerulus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Multiple collecting ducts converge at a renal papilla, the apex of a renal pyramid, which drains urine into a minor calyx. — Slide 41 (Collecting Tubule & Ducts)"},{"q":"Each limb of the loop of Henle has a characteristic wall thickness pattern. The descending limb is:","choices":["Thick then thin, projecting toward the medulla","Thin then thick, returning to the cortex","Uniformly thick along its entire length","Uniformly thin along its entire length"],"answer":0,"exp":"The descending limb runs thick then thin as it projects toward the medulla; the ascending limb runs thin then thick as it returns to the cortex. — Slide 39 (Loop of Henle)"},{"q":"In females, which organ lies posterosuperior to the urinary bladder?","choices":["The uterus","The rectum","The prostate","The sigmoid colon"],"answer":0,"exp":"In females, the uterus lies posterosuperior to the bladder, with the vagina posteroinferior. — Slide 51 (Urinary Bladder)"},{"q":"In males, which gland lies immediately inferior to the urinary bladder?","choices":["The adrenal gland","The seminal gland","The bulbo-urethral gland","The prostate gland"],"answer":3,"exp":"In males, the prostate gland lies immediately inferior to the bladder, while the rectum lies posterosuperior. — Slide 51 (Urinary Bladder)"},{"q":"The urinary bladder lies immediately superior and posterior to which bony landmark?","choices":["The pubic symphysis","The sacral promontory","The ischial tuberosity","The iliac crest"],"answer":0,"exp":"The bladder lies immediately superior and posterior to the pubic symphysis. — Slide 49 (Bladder)"},{"q":"The median umbilical ligament, related to the bladder apex, is a remnant of the:","choices":["Ureteric bud","Ductus deferens","Round ligament","Urachus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The median umbilical ligament is the fibrous remnant of the urachus, the fetal tube that carried urine to the umbilical cord. — Slide 49 (Bladder)"},{"q":"The ureters average approximately what length as they run retroperitoneally from the renal pelvis to the bladder?","choices":["10 cm","25 cm","50 cm","5 cm"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ureters average about 25 cm in length as they run retroperitoneally from the renal pelvis to the bladder. — Slide 44 (Ureters)"},{"q":"The detrusor muscle of the bladder wall is composed of which tissue arranged in three layers?","choices":["Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle","Transitional epithelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"The detrusor muscle is smooth muscle arranged in three layers (inner and outer longitudinal around a middle circular layer). — Slide 53 (Bladder)"},{"q":"The muscularis of the ureter consists of smooth muscle whose peristaltic action moves urine. Its basic arrangement is:","choices":["Inner longitudinal and outer circular","Inner circular and outer longitudinal only","A single circular layer","Skeletal muscle bundles"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ureteral muscularis is arranged as inner longitudinal and outer circular smooth muscle (plus an added outer longitudinal layer in the distal third), producing peristalsis. — Slide 45 (Ureter Walls)"},{"q":"The fenestrated endothelium of the glomerular capillaries is significant because the fenestrae make the capillaries:","choices":["Impermeable to water and small solutes","Highly permeable for filtration","Contractile like vascular smooth muscle","Rich in cilia that propel the filtrate"],"answer":1,"exp":"The large fenestrae (pores) of the glomerular endothelium make the capillaries highly permeable, facilitating rapid filtration of plasma. — Slide 33 (Filtration Membranes)"},{"q":"Filtration slits, which help prevent large anions and proteins from being filtered, are formed by the pedicels of which cells?","choices":["Macula densa cells","Podocytes","Juxtaglomerular cells","Simple squamous parietal cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Podocyte pedicels (foot processes) interdigitate to form the filtration slits that restrict passage of large anions and proteins. — Slide 34 (Filtration Membranes)"},{"q":"The macula densa, part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus, functions as a sensor for:","choices":["Sodium (NaCl) concentration and tubular flow","Oxygen tension in the renal parenchyma only","Blood pH within the afferent arteriole only","Glucose concentration in the tubular filtrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"The macula densa senses NaCl concentration and tubular flow, signaling the juxtaglomerular (granular) cells to release renin. — Slide 36 (Juxtaglomerular Apparatus)"},{"q":"On average, the fully distended adult bladder can hold approximately how much urine?","choices":["About 2 liters","About 100 mL","About 500 mL","About 50 mL"],"answer":2,"exp":"The fully distended adult bladder can hold approximately 500 mL, expanding to about 15 times its empty volume. — Slide 53 (Bladder)"},{"q":"The male urethra differs from the female urethra in length, measuring approximately:","choices":["3–4 cm (vs 18–20 cm in females)","18–20 cm (vs 3–4 cm in females)","25 cm (vs 10 cm in females)","1 cm (vs 5 cm in females)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The male urethra is about 18–20 cm long (with prostatic, membranous, and penile regions), while the female urethra is about 3–4 cm long. — Slides 58–59 (Urethra)"},{"q":"Excitation of the renal sympathetic nerves affects the glomerulus by:","choices":["Dilating the afferent arteriole to substantially boost glomerular filtration","Vasoconstricting both afferent and efferent arterioles, decreasing urine production","Directly secreting renin into the bloodstream without any intermediary","Relaxing the detrusor muscle of the bladder to permit filling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic excitation vasoconstricts both the afferent and efferent arterioles, reducing glomerular blood flow and decreasing urine production. — Slide 26 (Innervation)"},{"q":"The apex of a renal pyramid, which projects toward a minor calyx, is the:","choices":["Renal hilum","Renal column","Renal papilla","Renal arch"],"answer":2,"exp":"The apex of each renal pyramid is the renal papilla, which projects into and drains urine toward a minor calyx. — Slide 14 (Coronal Plane Sections)"}]};
